  <title>Tax exemptions</title>

  <p>A tax exemption is a tax rate marked as an exemption in
  <link xref="tax_rates"/>. Once a customer has it, it can be chosen on their
  invoices. There are two windows for exemptions: one opened from the invoice,
  and one from the contacts window that keeps the signed form.</p>

  <section id="invoice">
  <title>From the invoice</title>
  <p>Click <gui>Tax exemption</gui> on the <link xref="invoice">invoice</link>.
  The <gui>Customer Tax Exemptions</gui> window lists every exemption.</p>
  <steps>
    <item><p>Check <gui>Available</gui> beside the exemption the customer
    has.</p></item>
    <item><p>If the tax rate has an exemption template, the form opens in
    LibreOffice already filled in with the customer and your company, ready to
    print and sign.</p></item>
    <item><p>Close the window, and choose the exemption in the box beside
    <gui>Tax exemption</gui> on the invoice.</p></item>
  </steps>
  <p><gui>Regenerate Exemption</gui> opens the form again for the selected
  exemption. An exemption cannot be taken away from a customer here:
  unchecking <gui>Available</gui> is refused.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
  <title>From the contacts window</title>
  <p>Select the contact in <link xref="contacts"/> and click <gui>Contact
  exemptions</gui>. This window keeps a copy of each signed exemption form.
  Changes are saved as they are made.</p>
  <steps>
    <item><p>Select the exemption.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click <gui>Add exemption</gui> and scan or choose the signed form;
    see <link xref="pdf_attachment"/>. The exemption becomes
    <gui>Available</gui> to the customer, and <gui>File scanned</gui> is
    checked.</p></item>
  </steps>
  <p><gui>View exemption</gui> opens the form kept for the selected exemption.
  Only one person can have a contact's exemptions open at a time; anyone else
  is told that somebody else is editing them.</p>
  </section>

</page>
